**Inheritance.** The SDK's group description states the rule: all account identities can be group members, and members inherit the permissions assigned to their group. Groups can contain other groups, so Maya's path is Maya → plant-eu-analysts → quality-analysts → grant. The same page calls assigning workspace access and Unity Catalog access-control policies to groups, not individuals, best practice.

```sql
-- Synthetic teaching example; not executed.
GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG quality TO `quality-analysts`;
GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA quality.accepted TO `quality-analysts`;
GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA quality.accepted TO `quality-analysts`;
SHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA quality.accepted;
```

SELECT on the schema reaches every current and future table in it, which is right only because every accepted table is meant for analysts; the privileges reference owns the exact inheritance rules.

**Account groups and workspace-local groups.** The provider manages both kinds. Account groups are the ones SCIM maintains and the ones this module grants to; workspace-local groups exist in one workspace only and are documented on their own page, so check it before relying on one for Unity Catalog access.

**Who changes membership.** If the identity provider owns a group through SCIM, membership changes happen there and flow in on the next sync; an in-workspace edit may be overwritten. Account access rules also define a group manager role. Membership is therefore an access decision: the person who can add members to quality-analysts can grant read on the accepted schema without touching a GRANT statement.

**Limits and failure modes.** Direct user grants linger after groups arrive and keep access alive after a move. Deep nesting hides paths; review effective access with the real principal. Sync is periodic, so a move is not instant. Entitlements such as workspace access are separate from catalog privileges.
